To remove leading whitespace from a string in Java, you can use the `trim()` method or regular expressions.

Here are some ways to achieve this:

**Method 1: Using `trim()`**
```java
public class Main {
    public static String stripLeadingWhitespace(String input) {
        return input.trim();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(stripLeadingWhitespace("   Hello World  "));
    }
}
```
This method is simple and efficient, but it only removes whitespace from the beginning of the string.

**Method 2: Using regular expressions**
```java
public class Main {
    public static String stripLeadingWhitespace(String input) {
        return input.replaceAll("^\\s+", "");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(stripLeadingWhitespace("   Hello World  "));
    }
}
```
This method uses a regular expression to match one or more whitespace characters at the beginning of the string and replaces them with an empty string.

**Method 3: Using `String.replaceAll()` with a specific pattern**
```java
public class Main {
    public static String stripLeadingWhitespace(String input) {
        return input.replaceAll("^\\s+", "");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(stripLeadingWhitespace("   Hello World  "));
    }
}
```
This method is similar to the previous one, but it uses a specific pattern `^\\s+` to match leading whitespace.

All three methods will produce the same result: a string with leading whitespace removed.
